# Accepted misconfiguration findings.
#
# Each entry is a conscious trade-off, not a silenced alarm. The reasoning also
# lives inline next to the resource it applies to; this file is what trivy
# actually reads. Anything added here needs a justification good enough to say
# out loud in a review.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0104 (CRITICAL) — Security group rule allows unrestricted egress
#
# modules/compute: the ingest task talks to sec.gov plus S3, Secrets Manager and
# SSM. None of those publish stable CIDRs — an allowlist breaks whenever SEC's
# CDN moves, and pinning AWS ranges properly would mean VPC endpoints at roughly
# $7/month each for a batch job that runs 25 minutes a day.
#
# The control that actually matters here is inbound, and this security group has
# NO ingress rules at all. Egress-only from a task that starts, works and exits.
AWS-0104

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0345 (HIGH) — IAM role uses a policy that allows 's3:*'
#
# modules/iam, the Terraform provisioning role only. It creates and destroys the
# project's buckets, so it cannot be scoped to ARNs that do not exist until it
# makes them. Blast radius is bounded elsewhere: the trust policy admits only
# the account root and repo 2's CI role, and the raw bucket's own policy denies
# deletes to every principal except this one.
#
# Note the ingest task role — the one that actually runs untrusted-ish code — is
# scoped to s3:PutObject on a single prefix and is not covered by this ignore.
AWS-0345

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0132 (HIGH) ×2 — Bucket does not encrypt data with a customer managed key
#
# modules/storage, both buckets. They ARE encrypted at rest with SSE-S3
# (AES256); the finding is about who owns the key, not whether encryption
# exists. A customer-managed KMS key costs $1/month each plus per-request
# charges, and two would roughly double this project's entire ~$1-3/month bill.
#
# The data is public SEC filings. There is no regulatory requirement for
# customer-held keys here. A compliance context would change this decision and
# nothing else about the configuration.
AWS-0132
